chive.org///web/20170511024849/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MUSI/UN3321-20171-001/"},{"code":"MUSI UN3322","title":"MUSIC THEORY IV","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: \nMUSI V3321\n. \nCorequisites: one course from Ear-Training I-IV (\nV2314\n, \nV2315\n, \nV3316\n, or \nV3317\n, as determined by placement exam.) \n Intermediate analysis and composition in a variety of tonal and extended tonal idioms. (Through Spring 2014, this course was entitled Chromatic Harmony and Counterpoint II.) A one-hour weekly lab is required, to be scheduled at the beginning of the term.","dept":"Music","deptCode":"MUSI","instructor":"Joseph P Dubiel","culpa":3,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2,4],"start":790,"end":865,"startLabel":"1:10pm","endLabel":"2:25pm","location":"620 Dodge Hall","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["MUSI V3321","V2314"],["V2315"],["V3316","V3317"]],"callNumber":"23830","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"UN3322-20171-002","openTo":["Barnard","Columbia College","Engineering and Applied Science: Undergraduate","General Studies","Global Programs","School of Professional Studies"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20170511024851/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MUSI/UN3322-20171-002/"},{"code":"MUSI UN3322","title":"MUSIC THEORY IV","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: \nMUSI V3321\n. \nCorequisites: one course from Ear-Training I-IV (\nV2314\n, \nV2315\n, \nV3316\n, or \nV3317\n, as determined by placement exam.) \n Intermediate analysis and composition in a variety of tonal and extended tonal idioms. (Through Spring 2014, this course was entitled Chromatic Harmony and Counterpoint II.) A one-hour weekly lab is required, to be scheduled at the beginning of the term.","dept":"Music","deptCode":"MUSI","instructor":"Ellie Hisama","culpa":2,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellie_Hisama","days":[1,3],"start":790,"end":865,"startLabel":"1:10pm","endLabel":"2:25pm","location":"620 Dodge Hall","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["MUSI V3321","V2314"],["V2315"],["V3316","V3317"]],"callNumber":"68891","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"UN3322-20171-001","openTo":["Barnard","Columbia College","Engineering and Applied Science: Undergraduate","General Studies","Global Programs","School of Professional Studies"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20170511024850/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MUSI/UN3322-20171-001/"},{"code":"MUSI UN3338","title":"MUSIC & TECH CRITICAL PERSPECTIVE","subtitle":"MUSIC & TECH CRIT PERSPECTIVE","desc":"Consider a song on the radio. The musicians are performing on instruments, which are recorded with microphones, into computer software that converts sound into data, at which point the data is processed according to the tastes of the artist and producerâ€”tastes shaped, no doubt, by the array of processing software available to them in the first place. After that, the finished product is printed to tape or disc, circulated at various compression rates online, further processed by the radio station, and consumed on everything from car speakers to $1,000 headphones to iPod earbuds. To an extent perhaps only marginally impacted by the content of the song, by the time this song on the radio gets to our ears technology has exerted a huge influence on the sonic character, and thus our experience, of the music. Further, all of these technologies and techniques have complex histories and social ","dept":"Music","deptCode":"MUSI","instructor":"William L Mason","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1,3],"start":880,"end":955,"startLabel":"2:40pm","endLabel":"3:55pm","location":"620 Dodge Hall","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"62146","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"UN3338-20171-001","openTo":["Barnard","Columbia College","Engineering and Applied Science: Undergraduate","General Studies"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20170511024852/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MUSI/UN3338-20171-001/"},{"code":"MUSI UN3400","title":"TOPICS IN MUSIC & SOCIETY","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course seeks to approach the study of music and society by comparatively studying repertories from different parts of the world, how the history of ideas and methods of studying such repertoires shaped them, the practices that constitute them and the ways they are understood and used by different peoples.  Central to this course is the interrelationship between the constitution of a repertoire and the history of the construction of knowledge about it.","dept":"Music","deptCode":"MUSI","instructor":"Martha K Newland","culpa":3,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2,4],"start":700,"end":775,"startLabel":"11:40am","endLabel":"12:55pm","location":"404 Dodge Hall","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"18062","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"UN3400-20171-001","openTo":["Barnard","Columbia College","Engineering and Applied Science: Undergraduate","General Studies","Global Programs","School of Professional Studies"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20170511024853/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MUSI/UN3400-20171-001/"},{"code":"MUSI UN3516","title":"ADVANCED PIANO INSTRUCTION II","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: Instructor permission. \n $500 MUSIC LESSON FEE FOR NON-MAJORS/CONCENTRATORS. All freshmen and sophomore students wishing to major/minor/concentrate in music, and thereby get their lesson fees waived, are required to make an appointment with Prof. Brad Garton ( *Special j azz concentrators will meet with Prof. Chris Washburne), Dean of Undergraduate Studies, \nEVERY SEMESTER \nduring the registration period , in order to register for appropriate courses . Fees will not be waived without meeting this requirement. All accepted MPP students must register for lessons and ensembles by the change-of-program deadline in order to be allowed to attend lessons that semester. Petitioning students must notify MPP staff prior to this deadline.","dept":"Music","deptCode":"MUSI","instructor":"Reiko Uchida","culpa":2,"wikipedia":null,"days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"startLabel":null,"endLabel":null,"location":null,"campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1,"pointsLabel":"1","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"16593","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"UN3516-20171-103","openTo":["Barnard","Columbia College","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Engineering and Applied Science: Undergraduate","General Studies","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","School of Professional Studies","School of the Arts"],"type":"STUDIO","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20170511024859/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MUSI/UN3516-20171-103/"},{"code":"MUSI UN3516","title":"ADVANCED PIANO INSTRUCTION II","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: Instructor permission. \n $500 MUSIC LESSON FEE FOR NON-MAJORS/CONCENTRATORS. All freshmen and sophomore students wishing to major/minor/concentrate in music, and thereby get their lesson fees waived, are required to make an appointment with Prof. Brad Garton ( *Special j azz concentrators will meet with Prof. Chris Washburne), Dean of Undergraduate Studies, \nEVERY SEMESTER \nduring the registration period , in order to register for appropriate courses . Fees will not be waived without meeting this requirement. All accepted MPP students must register for lessons and ensembles by the change-of-program deadline in order to be allowed to attend lessons that semester. Petitioning students must notify MPP sta